"-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cde51993-46ba-5136-b04b-e60fd29425c0" :answer/id #uuid "cde51993-46ba-5136-b04b-e60fd29425c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19e3672d-b2c0-5d72-8182-458842a8082b" :answer-field/id #uuid "19e3672d-b2c0-5d72-8182-458842a8082b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-5e6fc4ae-7a22-54f3-b550-cb820c80a006" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5e6fc4ae-7a22-54f3-b550-cb820c80a006" "answer-d46a64ba-9ced-58f0-be3d-8e24b4b25a3b" "answer-a578b96c-4dcd-5d98-afab-f309822e5646" "answer-e113409c-949f-5f4f-94e4-46ddd49d56bd" "answer-275267aa-7fe6-5509-801e-f9b125fe4ce0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e6fc4ae-7a22-54f3-b550-cb820c80a006" :answer/id #uuid "5e6fc4ae-7a22-54f3-b550-cb820c80a006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(B)=-1\\in B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d46a64ba-9ced-58f0-be3d-8e24b4b25a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d46a64ba-9ced-58f0-be3d-8e24b4b25a3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(B)=-1\\notin B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a578b96c-4dcd-5d98-afab-f309822e5646" :answer/id #uuid "a578b96c-4dcd-5d98-afab-f309822e5646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(B)=2\\in B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e113409c-949f-5f4f-94e4-46ddd49d56bd" :answer/id #uuid "e113409c-949f-5f4f-94e4-46ddd49d56bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(B)=2\\notin B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-275267aa-7fe6-5509-801e-f9b125fe4ce0" :answer/id #uuid "275267aa-7fe6-5509-801e-f9b125fe4ce0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$inf(B)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162530 :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{1+3{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=1+3{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/19/198c3d6737439fcfa204926394b317aae0026502b5eaacb9e2f0429941f23db3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $1.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=1+3{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{7}{4}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186160502 :answer-field/correct "answer-128ee1a2-ad98-5075-846f-b8cbe5627a55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdb9e03b-cb3d-5a8d-9ae0-14d4a6eba10a" "answer-7d320ab4-e7ea-5b1a-9b1e-fcd8c46760b7" "answer-128ee1a2-ad98-5075-846f-b8cbe5627a55" "answer-ec2c2649-8169-5a93-bf6e-55c2f61519ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdb9e03b-cb3d-5a8d-9ae0-14d4a6eba10a" :answer/id #uuid "fdb9e03b-cb3d-5a8d-9ae0-14d4a6eba10a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d320ab4-e7ea-5b1a-9b1e-fcd8c46760b7" :answer/id #uuid "7d320ab4-e7ea-5b1a-9b1e-fcd8c46760b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-128ee1a2-ad98-5075-846f-b8cbe5627a55" :answer/id #uuid "128ee1a2-ad98-5075-846f-b8cbe5627a55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec2c2649-8169-5a93-bf6e-55c2f61519ac" :answer/id #uuid "ec2c2649-8169-5a93-bf6e-55c2f61519ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{7}{4}\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d021d0f6-fbf3-5eed-804d-eee551071be6" :question/id #uuid "d021d0f6-fbf3-5eed-804d-eee551071be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-253911" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1)\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1),\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/58/5812e3dd22994d613ecc84087418cddfa978e98c1324869f290e1bf2b1317bad.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating, non-convergent sequence. Moreover, $A$ is unbounded above. In other words, for any real number $x,$ we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ such that ${a}_{n}$ is greater than $x.$ Therefore, $A$ has no upper bounds and $sup(A)=+\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afd78a1e-c9e7-53fa-8911-aad04834878d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afd78a1e-c9e7-53fa-8911-aad04834878d" :answer-field/id #uuid "afd78a1e-c9e7-53fa-8911-aad04834878d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c14e4e69-3b50-5f9c-aab3-933c64469cdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c14e4e69-3b50-5f9c-aab3-933c64469cdd" "answer-b1f6d5db-d453-51b2-81bf-72bad96c4aaa" "answer-c8035750-8d78-5097-9367-a314cfe52d58" "answer-9ac9b3db-343c-58f0-83db-0523448093be" "answer-c152b716-01ac-5012-a5ba-cb9700cb7912"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c14e4e69-3b50-5f9c-aab3-933c64469cdd" :answer/id #uuid "c14e4e69-3b50-5f9c-aab3-933c64469cdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=+\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1f6d5db-d453-51b2-81bf-72bad96c4aaa" :answer/id #uuid "b1f6d5db-d453-51b2-81bf-72bad96c4aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=0\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8035750-8d78-5097-9367-a314cfe52d58" :answer/id #uuid "c8035750-8d78-5097-9367-a314cfe52d58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=0\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ac9b3db-343c-58f0-83db-0523448093be" :answer/id #uuid "9ac9b3db-343c-58f0-83db-0523448093be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\in A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c152b716-01ac-5012-a5ba-cb9700cb7912" :answer/id #uuid "c152b716-01ac-5012-a5ba-cb9700cb7912" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\notin A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162531 :question/problem "![](images/73/73d739c3d2e4c6bc9d296136ef6f17e7fa0bf565aa4c8c4712e54df4e952935f.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-1,3].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-1,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186160508 :answer-field/correct "answer-43d3c0fd-5ae1-5420-a4ca-ca1916926180" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43d3c0fd-5ae1-5420-a4ca-ca1916926180"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43d3c0fd-5ae1-5420-a4ca-ca1916926180" :answer/id #uuid "43d3c0fd-5ae1-5420-a4ca-ca1916926180" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-75529c50-5084-5200-a1d5-34d0fbf018a7" :question/id #uuid "75529c50-5084-5200-a1d5-34d0fbf018a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-253141" :question/problem "![](images/81/8152677cc952ca219aa9395f3d1a9acbc9379b1cffd0b0f0e6bd0f3f0148632a.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d5dd2e68-c307-5896-9e28-9a0db4658a38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d5dd2e68-c307-5896-9e28-9a0db4658a38" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5dd2e68-c307-5896-9e28-9a0db4658a38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-2439eeb1-f067-5723-ba6c-598c5d8955f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2439eeb1-f067-5723-ba6c-598c5d8955f2" "answer-f5c1f462-2a1b-56df-a334-da05637bfe75" "answer-8d3302b8-4e4c-524a-9fcb-03282d5609f6" "answer-b1af54c6-96ae-5f04-9cc5-657032bf0dd7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2439eeb1-f067-5723-ba6c-598c5d8955f2" :answer/id #uuid "2439eeb1-f067-5723-ba6c-598c5d8955f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5c1f462-2a1b-56df-a334-da05637bfe75" :answer/id #uuid "f5c1f462-2a1b-56df-a334-da05637bfe75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d3302b8-4e4c-524a-9fcb-03282d5609f6" :answer/id #uuid "8d3302b8-4e4c-524a-9fcb-03282d5609f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1af54c6-96ae-5f04-9cc5-657032bf0dd7" :answer/id #uuid "b1af54c6-96ae-5f04-9cc5-657032bf0dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-56f49ac3-e6b5-56aa-af36-053b89a652ef" :question/id #uuid "56f49ac3-e6b5-56aa-af36-053b89a652ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-253900" :question/problem "![](images/40/40c6f3bbfcbf08db07501076a43ba011da20c57c70865ec2700334d560936eae.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $y$ for any $y>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d47acad2-139e-5bc5-9fa6-bc7c2c008592"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d47acad2-139e-5bc5-9fa6-bc7c2c008592" :answer-field/id #uuid "d47acad2-139e-5bc5-9fa6-bc7c2c008592" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c2af8039-a78b-5fde-b644-dab8ea6d11a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2af8039-a78b-5fde-b644-dab8ea6d11a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2af8039-a78b-5fde-b644-dab8ea6d11a7" :answer/id #uuid "c2af8039-a78b-5fde-b644-dab8ea6d11a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-28cb24da-7ead-51cb-82fe-c700e5c2c25f" :question/id #uuid "28cb24da-7ead-51cb-82fe-c700e5c2c25f" :question/math-academy-id "q-253865" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e39b8db15f47b0c07efec1b364f6fda7b3b7991ba13d71125505b1172937888.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is smaller than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=-\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3798da6-3e26-54e4-8f7c-6af936e37df8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3798da6-3e26-54e4-8f7c-6af936e37df8" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3798da6-3e26-54e4-8f7c-6af936e37df8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8d8029f6-c90e-5c0c-bfd4-81655fc8e004" :answer-field/choices ["answer-712bb123-f03c-5bbb-9049-2ae6f776e6a5" "answer-a7ab285f-819b-5164-92e2-6ee90a76aa82" "answer-8d8029f6-c90e-5c0c-bfd4-81655fc8e004" "answer-4cc0bbd3-ba25-5fd0-9b64-f33746e77c6b" "answer-72410791-1a6d-5c90-b6f7-7312d8ca4da6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-712bb123-f03c-5bbb-9049-2ae6f776e6a5" :answer/id #uuid "712bb123-f03c-5bbb-9049-2ae6f776e6a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7ab285f-819b-5164-92e2-6ee90a76aa82" :answer/id #uuid "a7ab285f-819b-5164-92e2-6ee90a76aa82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d8029f6-c90e-5c0c-bfd4-81655fc8e004" :answer/id #uuid "8d8029f6-c90e-5c0c-bfd4-81655fc8e004" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cc0bbd3-ba25-5fd0-9b64-f33746e77c6b" :answer/id #uuid "4cc0bbd3-ba25-5fd0-9b64-f33746e77c6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72410791-1a6d-5c90-b6f7-7312d8ca4da6" :answer/id #uuid "72410791-1a6d-5c90-b6f7-7312d8ca4da6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ddf4ec2c-4f4d-56c1-bc14-78cfd2f52940" :question/id #uuid "ddf4ec2c-4f4d-56c1-bc14-78cfd2f52940" :question/math-academy-id "q-253895" :question/problem "![](images/50/50b52da574101dfca7c620411a446f2c5f3ef41242186ca38da072e140019024.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-2,3].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 3$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<3$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $3,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[3,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[3,\\infty )=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42f7eabb-2484-504e-a47e-b134986a6e92"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42f7eabb-2484-504e-a47e-b134986a6e92" :answer-field/id #uuid "42f7eabb-2484-504e-a47e-b134986a6e92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-67197de5-74fe-5dc0-abe6-0952b996a82d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67197de5-74fe-5dc0-abe6-0952b996a82d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67197de5-74fe-5dc0-abe6-0952b996a82d" :answer/id #uuid "67197de5-74fe-5dc0-abe6-0952b996a82d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186112800 :answer-field/choices 17592186112801] [:db/retract 17592186112800 :answer-field/choices 17592186112802] [:db/retract 17592186112800 :answer-field/choices 17592186112803] [:db/retract 17592186112800 :answer-field/choices 17592186112804] [:db/retract 17592186112800 :answer-field/choices 17592186112805] [:db/retract 17592186114337 :answer-field/choices 17592186114338] [:db/retract 17592186114337 :answer-field/choices 17592186114339] [:db/retract 17592186114337 :answer-field/choices 17592186114340] [:db/retract 17592186114337 :answer-field/choices 17592186114341] [:db/retract 17592186114337 :answer-field/choices 17592186114342] {:db/id 17592186115406 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-589a66a6-0e3d-5725-8cc4-53657e5b6c5b" "question-1c3543cf-d292-519c-b94c-943e42d7f653"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186114982 :question/worked-solution "In the triangle we see two congruent sides,\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC},\n$$\n\nbut the third side is not congruent:\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\not\\cong \\overline{BC}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since only two sides are congruent, $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{BC}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115407 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-78cb72f5-fa99-5a05-bb80-ea35eabe4485"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186114983 :question/problem "Given a triangle ![](images/33/335d1d0e07d6f4a7f3daaa9458a221cb5eebc714866d6494d310699b5b62d78e.svg), we know that ![](images/0d/0d097e17cb3fe4b7dbb225dcbea036db715b976ee6728b0e5709b55ce9100e51.svg) and ![](images/f6/f6ff20d28baf805ac27eb6a7822291c152a9b44e6b0743a1a7fe59635bfb5528.svg) Based on the angles, which type of triangle is ![](images/af/afa0d0e402ca98fc0a1a940db76dd0e08071e0ad436191b596e0a6fb14e69027.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) be the measure of ![](images/88/886c073571c9bd4fcf1515db8eda5f14ad06f8eb1a7b0612410eb48e7e744a2a.svg) Then ![](images/0f/0f90935af5a2c2117a80eadb5bbd88380726fd68fc830df2d81e3b3b4a99a242.svg)\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is ![](images/47/4725d33446084ddb65f6736ea0750a68503811aff88fa69e321dee24002e95e4.svg), we have \n![](images/ca/cad788019a25c5b35243f9b4613b75863be29389a9cc71f0708e3235e3300bf2.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/4f/4f435d11ba07ac4cd7b06579abd2b5a29657db4e2b6c3ce097e9f6df0907e6fd.svg) and ![](images/a7/a77859d66a33dd7ee085d5eea33781e4aadba4d93f2c5cc99cef1e9a128d3b8e.svg) \n\nSo, ![](images/14/141e6fbb250a22f9c3e054f7bebb3a7e071a1b108418aa8275aac87fa2027b80.svg) is an obtuse triangle because it has an obtuse angle (![](images/d5/d543d4ca81776165911a27bbf1cba5e8265c99a599fa8df4e11bad00e10c4903.svg))." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115408 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5ebcfbce-bc83-5ed3-8c76-d526d36ab2c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186114984 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the missing angle $\\angle A.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+{80}^{∘}+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{155}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={25}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m\\angle A <      m\\angle C <      m\\angle B.$\n\nSince $\\angle B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{CA}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $\\angle A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC < BA < CA\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overline{BC}, \\overline{BA}, \\overline{CA}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115224 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4b8c6582-8730-58e4-8a1f-1712623b6eac" "question-72d7422a-3ddb-5828-b3d9-7f65634fb116"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115054 :question/worked-solution "The shortest side is $\\overline{BC},$ so the smallest interior angle is $\\angle{A}.$\n\nThe longest side is $\\overline{AB},$ which means that the opposite angle, $\\angle C$, must be the largest one.\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n\n$$\n\\angle A,\\,  \\angle B,\\,  \\angle C.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-589a66a6-0e3d-5725-8cc4-53657e5b6c5b" :question/id #uuid "589a66a6-0e3d-5725-8cc4-53657e5b6c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77588" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d828b48f347d79f8a6536ca178f5bb24bd46f6d35c278ca21bebdbb9a1b86750.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements best describes the triangle above?" :question/worked-solution "In the above triangle, we see two congruent sides,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{AB}≅\\overset{―}{BC},\n$$\n\nbut the third side is not congruent:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC}≇\\overset{―}{AC}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since only two sides are congruent, $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AC}.$\n\nOn the other hand, $∠B$ is a right angle. Therefore, $△ABC$ is a right isosceles triangle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ada43638-a4e1-5ad8-becd-025cb0438ee3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ada43638-a4e1-5ad8-becd-025cb0438ee3" :answer-field/id #uuid "ada43638-a4e1-5ad8-becd-025cb0438ee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd163e44-88cd-534a-9017-a36ba40d6399" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd163e44-88cd-534a-9017-a36ba40d6399" "answer-de081eb0-46a5-549d-8316-e4c5f8a031ab" "answer-172061d0-77ef-5f1d-af93-07de178116b1" "answer-ec652361-e551-578a-84b0-48b5e30c777c" "answer-6a868207-b963-5d3a-9c98-f52a012f0ae5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd163e44-88cd-534a-9017-a36ba40d6399" :answer/id #uuid "dd163e44-88cd-534a-9017-a36ba40d6399" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is a right isosceles triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de081eb0-46a5-549d-8316-e4c5f8a031ab" :answer/id #uuid "de081eb0-46a5-549d-8316-e4c5f8a031ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is a right scalene triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-172061d0-77ef-5f1d-af93-07de178116b1" :answer/id #uuid "172061d0-77ef-5f1d-af93-07de178116b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is an equilateral triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec652361-e551-578a-84b0-48b5e30c777c" :answer/id #uuid "ec652361-e551-578a-84b0-48b5e30c777c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a868207-b963-5d3a-9c98-f52a012f0ae5" :answer/id #uuid "6a868207-b963-5d3a-9c98-f52a012f0ae5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{BC}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1c3543cf-d292-519c-b94c-943e42d7f653" :question/id #uuid "1c3543cf-d292-519c-b94c-943e42d7f653" :question/math-academy-id "q-77587" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b05ae89c373b6ce1ed9326099dd2f86f9d48220b36d29bf36d37a0c71d2ebaa9.png)\n\nClassify the triangle above." :question/worked-solution "In the above triangle, we see two congruent sides,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{AB}≅\\overset{―}{AC},\n$$\n\nbut the third side is not congruent:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{AB}≇\\overset{―}{BC}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since only two sides are congruent, $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{BC}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d32b6ff-f127-50bd-af43-06a5c09ffae0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d32b6ff-f127-50bd-af43-06a5c09ffae0" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d32b6ff-f127-50bd-af43-06a5c09ffae0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0a67eb31-ab29-5192-8e8e-efd79c92d8fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38d24690-6a46-553f-b9f2-e86abf0ae288" "answer-b5ef5b5b-e124-5b7f-93ba-e58f18e74a2c" "answer-2f7a4a80-ee23-5f72-b50b-fa531bef3203" "answer-0e043922-2590-5988-985e-ec5cfef1cb8d" "answer-0a67eb31-ab29-5192-8e8e-efd79c92d8fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38d24690-6a46-553f-b9f2-e86abf0ae288" :answer/id #uuid "38d24690-6a46-553f-b9f2-e86abf0ae288" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is a scalene triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5ef5b5b-e124-5b7f-93ba-e58f18e74a2c" :answer/id #uuid "b5ef5b5b-e124-5b7f-93ba-e58f18e74a2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is an equilateral triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f7a4a80-ee23-5f72-b50b-fa531bef3203" :answer/id #uuid "2f7a4a80-ee23-5f72-b50b-fa531bef3203" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e043922-2590-5988-985e-ec5cfef1cb8d" :answer/id #uuid "0e043922-2590-5988-985e-ec5cfef1cb8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AC}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a67eb31-ab29-5192-8e8e-efd79c92d8fe" :answer/id #uuid "0a67eb31-ab29-5192-8e8e-efd79c92d8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$△ABC$ is is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{BC}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115639 :question/problem "Given a triangle $△ABC$, we know that $m∠B$ is ${10}^{∘}$ less than $m∠A$ and ${40}^{∘}$ more than $m∠C.$ \n Based on the angles, which of the following statements best describes the triangle $△ABC?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the measure of $∠C.$ Then \n\nFrom the given information we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & =x+{40}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & =m∠B+{10}^{∘}=x+{50}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is ${180}^{∘}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (x+{50}^{∘})+(x+{40}^{∘})+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & ={30}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}={80}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠B & ={30}^{∘}+{40}^{∘}={70}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠C & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $ABC$ is an acute triangle because all three angles are acute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186112800 :answer-field/correct "answer-d806c811-ffd9-55db-a30e-683106aeb5fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-607c8806-dc33-5802-9c08-518552228347" "answer-4d1f7ff3-f5c1-59b7-b19f-089459911a32" "answer-d806c811-ffd9-55db-a30e-683106aeb5fc" "answer-0d740a44-5fdf-5115-b12e-fbcb42310ccb" "answer-73db0680-a903-5af9-9dd2-27e97bd5941a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-607c8806-dc33-5802-9c08-518552228347" :answer/id #uuid "607c8806-dc33-5802-9c08-518552228347" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={110}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d1f7ff3-f5c1-59b7-b19f-089459911a32" :answer/id #uuid "4d1f7ff3-f5c1-59b7-b19f-089459911a32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={30}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d806c811-ffd9-55db-a30e-683106aeb5fc" :answer/id #uuid "d806c811-ffd9-55db-a30e-683106aeb5fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={70}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d740a44-5fdf-5115-b12e-fbcb42310ccb" :answer/id #uuid "0d740a44-5fdf-5115-b12e-fbcb42310ccb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={80}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73db0680-a903-5af9-9dd2-27e97bd5941a" :answer/id #uuid "73db0680-a903-5af9-9dd2-27e97bd5941a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={90}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-78cb72f5-fa99-5a05-bb80-ea35eabe4485" :question/id #uuid "78cb72f5-fa99-5a05-bb80-ea35eabe4485" :question/math-academy-id "q-27488" :question/problem "Let $△ABC$ be a triangle such that $m∠A=2m∠C$ and  $m∠B=m∠A+m∠C.$ \nWhich of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the measure of the angle $∠C,$ then $m∠A=2x$ and $m∠B=2x+x=3x.$\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is ${180}^{∘}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+3x+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =2\\cdot {30}^{∘}={60}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠B & =3\\cdot {30}^{∘}={90}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠C & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $△ABC$ is a right triangle because it has a right angle ($m∠B={90}^{∘}$)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6cc75fa6-12d5-5223-9738-c28364a4f153"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6cc75fa6-12d5-5223-9738-c28364a4f153" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc75fa6-12d5-5223-9738-c28364a4f153" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e8eb6db-0234-5a72-be2e-24ab338a1f77" :answer-field/choices ["answer-528ef47f-3e72-58d7-9c9d-cd9c7a893107" "answer-e1bce8e3-015d-54a4-927a-762131b47801" "answer-e0adb8dd-6d79-55b7-8779-9fc7e836c6eb" "answer-c92f9e06-ec8a-55be-a541-69741620ec05" "answer-1e8eb6db-0234-5a72-be2e-24ab338a1f77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-528ef47f-3e72-58d7-9c9d-cd9c7a893107" :answer/id #uuid "528ef47f-3e72-58d7-9c9d-cd9c7a893107" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={120}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1bce8e3-015d-54a4-927a-762131b47801" :answer/id #uuid "e1bce8e3-015d-54a4-927a-762131b47801" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={120}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0adb8dd-6d79-55b7-8779-9fc7e836c6eb" :answer/id #uuid "e0adb8dd-6d79-55b7-8779-9fc7e836c6eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={70}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an acute triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c92f9e06-ec8a-55be-a541-69741620ec05" :answer/id #uuid "c92f9e06-ec8a-55be-a541-69741620ec05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={70}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is an obtuse triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e8eb6db-0234-5a72-be2e-24ab338a1f77" :answer/id #uuid "1e8eb6db-0234-5a72-be2e-24ab338a1f77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m∠B={90}^{∘},$ $△ABC$ is a right triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115900 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the missing angle $A.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{90}^{∘}+{46}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{136}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={44}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $∠A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC<AB<AC\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186114337 :answer-field/correct "answer-fd1499a7-15ed-58ad-a196-3a1befd0bcda" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f6857f4-dd41-503e-bd0c-5f034613f857" "answer-076bc481-a526-5ba8-8a57-a7b2f0af4833" "answer-663fd4ea-aa24-5d4c-84b8-2ebaa3d2b246" "answer-fd1499a7-15ed-58ad-a196-3a1befd0bcda" "answer-ee59f014-7b2f-5f34-a1f1-1a4fcbc99fbf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f6857f4-dd41-503e-bd0c-5f034613f857" :answer/id #uuid "5f6857f4-dd41-503e-bd0c-5f034613f857" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC},\\overset{―}{BC}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-076bc481-a526-5ba8-8a57-a7b2f0af4833" :answer/id #uuid "076bc481-a526-5ba8-8a57-a7b2f0af4833" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AC}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-663fd4ea-aa24-5d4c-84b8-2ebaa3d2b246" :answer/id #uuid "663fd4ea-aa24-5d4c-84b8-2ebaa3d2b246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{BC}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd1499a7-15ed-58ad-a196-3a1befd0bcda" :answer/id #uuid "fd1499a7-15ed-58ad-a196-3a1befd0bcda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee59f014-7b2f-5f34-a1f1-1a4fcbc99fbf" :answer/id #uuid "ee59f014-7b2f-5f34-a1f1-1a4fcbc99fbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AC},\\overset{―}{AB}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5ebcfbce-bc83-5ed3-8c76-d526d36ab2c8" :question/id #uuid "5ebcfbce-bc83-5ed3-8c76-d526d36ab2c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-67648" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6be28d1022cecbbd72a6dd6156f2bc2b6f653416607ae8fa409c25b3e97fdf35.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the missing angle $B.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+m∠B+{95}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{135}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠C$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{AB}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $∠A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC<AC<AB\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{AC},\\,\\overset{―}{AB}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5286c6e9-55fe-5c60-adb7-06c420bf40c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5286c6e9-55fe-5c60-adb7-06c420bf40c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "5286c6e9-55fe-5c60-adb7-06c420bf40c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-980782cb-3b75-5690-ab49-d7f64a28dfab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3492db71-3750-5a88-9806-0286938ed699" "answer-6c688e5c-eb7a-5410-87b3-42f348b01367" "answer-fee40f27-e957-5c7b-8e8a-853756e4eedc" "answer-1f0f8eeb-281c-5023-8994-95c153cefa9f" "answer-980782cb-3b75-5690-ab49-d7f64a28dfab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3492db71-3750-5a88-9806-0286938ed699" :answer/id #uuid "3492db71-3750-5a88-9806-0286938ed699" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AB},\\,\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{AC}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c688e5c-eb7a-5410-87b3-42f348b01367" :answer/id #uuid "6c688e5c-eb7a-5410-87b3-42f348b01367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AC},\\,\\overset{―}{AB},\\,\\overset{―}{BC}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fee40f27-e957-5c7b-8e8a-853756e4eedc" :answer/id #uuid "fee40f27-e957-5c7b-8e8a-853756e4eedc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AC},\\,\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{AB}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f0f8eeb-281c-5023-8994-95c153cefa9f" :answer/id #uuid "1f0f8eeb-281c-5023-8994-95c153cefa9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{AB},\\,\\overset{―}{AC}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-980782cb-3b75-5690-ab49-d7f64a28dfab" :answer/id #uuid "980782cb-3b75-5690-ab49-d7f64a28dfab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{AC},\\,\\overset{―}{AB}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b8c6582-8730-58e4-8a1f-1712623b6eac" :question/id #uuid "4b8c6582-8730-58e4-8a1f-1712623b6eac" :question/math-academy-id "q-67649" :question/problem "![](images/17/171cff06ab08f9ce49acd6b203c074ec329abdc35caca357221329980c85f531.png)\n\nOrder the angles of the triangle $△ABC$ from smallest to largest." :question/worked-solution "The shortest side is $\\overset{―}{AC},$ so the smallest interior angle is $∠B.$\n\nThe longest side is $\\overset{―}{BC},$ which means that the opposite angle, $∠A$, must be the largest one.\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n\n$$\n∠B,\\,∠C,\\,∠A.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8050e7c7-24fc-514d-abea-130031d44284"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8050e7c7-24fc-514d-abea-130031d44284" :answer-field/id #uuid "8050e7c7-24fc-514d-abea-130031d44284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9d4967cd-d07f-5f8f-b17c-148202bda167" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b730feb5-55fe-5332-bb20-b247934c7532" "answer-09cb2221-cd79-5d20-9c70-6c451fa4d6cb" "answer-9d4967cd-d07f-5f8f-b17c-148202bda167" "answer-37046b81-4949-5cbe-8e48-ca60345a5ea0" "answer-33c9a741-c7b5-5a92-8afd-3b8939d1607e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b730feb5-55fe-5332-bb20-b247934c7532" :answer/id #uuid "b730feb5-55fe-5332-bb20-b247934c7532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠A,\\,∠B,\\,∠C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09cb2221-cd79-5d20-9c70-6c451fa4d6cb" :answer/id #uuid "09cb2221-cd79-5d20-9c70-6c451fa4d6cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠B,\\,∠A,\\,∠C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d4967cd-d07f-5f8f-b17c-148202bda167" :answer/id #uuid "9d4967cd-d07f-5f8f-b17c-148202bda167" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠B,\\,∠C,\\,∠A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37046b81-4949-5cbe-8e48-ca60345a5ea0" :answer/id #uuid "37046b81-4949-5cbe-8e48-ca60345a5ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠C,\\,∠A,\\,∠B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33c9a741-c7b5-5a92-8afd-3b8939d1607e" :answer/id #uuid "33c9a741-c7b5-5a92-8afd-3b8939d1607e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠C,\\,∠B,\\,∠A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-72d7422a-3ddb-5828-b3d9-7f65634fb116" :question/id #uuid "72d7422a-3ddb-5828-b3d9-7f65634fb116" :question/math-academy-id "q-118189" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e7eb91b29c5a284d1ec7d487df5693dd5b10a18ff21dc1ef6223e47d32474e5b.png)\n\nOrder the angles of the triangle $△XYZ$ from smallest to largest." :question/worked-solution "The longest side is $\\overset{―}{XZ},$ which means that the opposite angle, $∠Y$, must be the largest one.\n\nThe shortest side is $\\overset{―}{YZ},$ so the smallest interior angle is $∠X.$\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n\n$$\n∠X,\\,∠Z,\\,∠Y\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ea43407-9f1e-56af-bd97-8affc0978055"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ea43407-9f1e-56af-bd97-8affc0978055" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea43407-9f1e-56af-bd97-8affc0978055" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7f516fb6-3127-5870-bfe6-21c7a1c8c830" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90dc1a14-4702-5ea6-a6fd-58af7310de63" "answer-7f516fb6-3127-5870-bfe6-21c7a1c8c830" "answer-f3b4e1f1-efbe-530d-bbb5-ad3959ec153e" "answer-c45f1f97-9f73-5aab-80b8-7dea64c363a1" "answer-bf7e1bb4-2dc6-584f-813a-1cdad8b9da7f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90dc1a14-4702-5ea6-a6fd-58af7310de63" :answer/id #uuid "90dc1a14-4702-5ea6-a6fd-58af7310de63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠X,\\,∠Y,\\,∠Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f516fb6-3127-5870-bfe6-21c7a1c8c830" :answer/id #uuid "7f516fb6-3127-5870-bfe6-21c7a1c8c830" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠X,\\,∠Z,\\,∠Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3b4e1f1-efbe-530d-bbb5-ad3959ec153e" :answer/id #uuid "f3b4e1f1-efbe-530d-bbb5-ad3959ec153e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠Y,\\,∠X,\\,∠Z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c45f1f97-9f73-5aab-80b8-7dea64c363a1" :answer/id #uuid "c45f1f97-9f73-5aab-80b8-7dea64c363a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠Y,\\,∠Z,\\,∠X" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf7e1bb4-2dc6-584f-813a-1cdad8b9da7f" :answer/id #uuid "bf7e1bb4-2dc6-584f-813a-1cdad8b9da7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "∠Z,\\,∠X,\\,∠Y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116446 :tutorial/content "Triangles can be classified by sides or by angles.\n\nFirst, let's consider the classifications by sides. We have three groups of triangles:\n\n- **Equilateral** triangles, where all sides have the same length.\n\n- **Isosceles** triangles, where only two sides are congruent. The congruent sides are called the **legs** of the triangle, and the third side is called the **base**.\n\n- **Scalene** triangles, where all sides have different measures.\n\n![](images/dc/dc38034d6c202b5168fc8184511a839eb41af206eb733e71e5f5c2fa970c0e33.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116447 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider classifying triangles by angles. We have three groups of triangles.\n\n- In an **acute** triangle, all three angles are acute.\n\n- A triangle with a right angle is called a **right** triangle.\n\n- A triangle with an obtuse angle is called an **obtuse** triangle.\n\n![](images/f1/f1c28d1f4b1ec7a93f1778e63be3727aa227e8eea5320eb7d5579c697e709429.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116448 :tutorial/content "Consider the following scalene triangle, where $AB=5$, $BC=7,$ and $AC=6$ units, respectively.\n\n![](images/57/57be69075640f742ffe6c004515485c4f479347f3b8b523272778948935048df.png)\n\nLooking at the angles, it's hard to tell which angle is the largest.\n\nHowever, since we know the sides, there is a simple rule that we can use to determine the relative size of the angles:\n\nLonger sides are opposite to larger angles and vice versa.\n\nApplying this rule to $\\triangle{ABC}$ depicted above, the longest side is $\\overline{BC}.$ Therefore, the largest angle is the angle opposite to $\\overline{BC},$ which is $\\color{red}\\angle{A}.$\n\n![](images/82/82d9ac6d61f285f3557d2dd0dda826803ffae306c5f9a927a422b3b953f262f4.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- the mid-sized side ($\\overline{AC}$) is opposite to the mid-sized angle ($\\angle B$), and\n\n- the shortest side ($\\overline{AB}$) is opposite to the smallest angle ($\\angle C$).\n\nSo, from least to greatest, the angles of the triangle are ordered as follows:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{C} < m\\angle{B} < m\\angle{A}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This rule works in reverse too. If you know the biggest angle of a triangle, the longest side is always opposite to this angle." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115429 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172050 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2d2171f1-1144-5e47-b93c-119cd4a73009" "question-29b45a36-fdc6-5e26-9a19-0e7651460f03" "question-a4b8bc46-7868-52e5-a7b3-40602e3b7509" "question-244776d4-c112-511f-a089-aa2567ad8e69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172051 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-353c7243-8109-54a9-8124-e25ba2a61d98" "question-db6c8509-7f18-5c65-821d-f6d09e6d4e13" "question-6a1d02fe-8171-5afa-b624-bbcbed75b5b0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172091 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7361f82f-c91c-52b9-80ec-852a74fe03bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171897 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: y-x\\geq 0\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: x^2+y^2> 4\\}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2d2171f1-1144-5e47-b93c-119cd4a73009" :question/id #uuid "2d2171f1-1144-5e47-b93c-119cd4a73009" :question/math-academy-id "q-98391" :question/problem "Which of the following regions is connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/08/0841ee8ea9f2d7454ee852041b3dfb470b6cf17237cec54960f400da337910c0.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-744523e1-233d-59ae-94be-8b4121363f99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-744523e1-233d-59ae-94be-8b4121363f99" :answer-field/id #uuid "744523e1-233d-59ae-94be-8b4121363f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6682f56e-33f7-5b51-acbd-732056a1aa84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6682f56e-33f7-5b51-acbd-732056a1aa84" "answer-96c839c4-93c2-5534-8309-489cf6547afc" "answer-0ef13c9c